00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.560
some background on
the types of tasks

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.520
you spend time on traditionally
when writing applications.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.320
Planning for the unknown
is something that's always

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.570
hard and full of guesses.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.880
One of the things that
you'll be working on

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.610
as you approach your
launch date will

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.340
be tasks like capacity
planning and provisioning

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.370
for your application
and database software.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.200
How big do you need
to make your VMs?

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.180
How many nodes do
you need to provision

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.430
to handle your
estimated traffic load?

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.290
How much storage do you need?

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.510
What about networking
connecting it all together?

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.450
How do you provision that?

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.740
There's a tenuous balance
you would traditionally

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.800
strike between
making sure you have

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.610
enough capacity available
to handle that expected load

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.650
and not having too
much that you're

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.115
wasting money on hardware
that you're not going to use.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:12.640
There's also operational aspects
you'll be working on as well.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.140
When you get to
your launch day, how

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.810
would you add more
capacity if you end up

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.020
being way larger than
you're expecting?

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.660
Or once you've
launched and you start

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.050
gaining traction and growing,
how do you grow capacity

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:28.800
in a more organic manner?

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.190
The other part of this
operational aspect

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.470
is working out how
you would roll out

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.950
updates to the application as
well as the database software

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.560
itself without having downtime.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.540
The other thing you end up
spending a bunch of time on

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.570
is making sure that you're
using in your database

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.070
in a way that scales.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.380
The last thing that
you want to find out

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.910
when you launch
your application is

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.240
you have a bunch of queries
that run in super linear time

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.420
compared to the data set.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.390
Queries can quickly degenerate
from taking milliseconds

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.240
to seconds, which ends up being
a very large problem when you

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.630
need to rethink your
data base model,

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:14.500
and then rebuild the
database to fix it.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.540
All of this is
essentially wasted time

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.390
that you could be spending
on actually writing

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.510
your application and
not spending time

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:28.010
on fixing and configuring your
applications infrastructure.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.240
So I'm definitely
not here to tell you

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.340
I can solve all these problems.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.380
But hopefully, I can
give you some insight

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.150
in how you could
use Cloud Datastore

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.310
and how you could
architect on top of it

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.680
to minimize most
of these problems.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.950
So let's start off with
a quick overview of what

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.462
Cloud Datastore is.

00:03:46.462 --> 00:03:47.920
I know some people
in this room may

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.870
have not heard about it before.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.650
So within the Google
Cloud platform

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:58.020
we have a portfolio of managed
database and storage solutions.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.750
As GCP is noSQL database
for use of facing data,

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.450
you typically see
Cloud Datastore

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.000
used for mobile and
web applications,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.640
rather than say, traditional
corporate back end

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:16.420
systems, or something like
internet of things device data.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.800
Now, there are always gray
areas where multiple database

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.850
and storage solutions will be
right for your application.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.740
As a general rule,
though, answering

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.750
some of these questions
can be a shortcut

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.870
to understand whether Cloud
Datastore is a solution for you

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:35.657
or perhaps some other solution.

00:04:35.657 --> 00:04:37.490
So the first thing you
want to ask yourself,

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.980
are you dealing with
semi-structured or structured

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.360
data?

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.390
Are you doing something that's
more transactional in nature

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:48.290
rather than analytical
and running reports?

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:52.610
And can you get away with
not doing complex joints

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.790
on your live data?

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:58.090
If you answered
yes to all of this,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.040
then it's a pretty
good probability

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.590
that Cloud Datastore is
a great solution for you.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:10.200
Now, Cloud Datastore is a
document oriented database

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.930
that's been used in
production by our customers

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.060
and Google itself for
over eight years now.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.640
Its real strong point is being
a fast, fully managed solution

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.530
that will scale from zero to
millions of requests per second

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.110
without you changing any
configuration, worrying

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.050
about adding nodes, and
without any downtime

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:34.250
to add this extra capacity.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:37.620
It literally scales from
handling your workload

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.446
on a single machine
to 10,000 of machines

00:05:41.446 --> 00:05:42.570
without you doing anything.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.990
What's really
beautiful about it is

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.960
under this
operational simplicity

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.980
is a really sophisticated,
synchronously replicated

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:55.880
database.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.250
We currently give you two
flavors of replication

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:02.360
you can choose depending on
the location that you select.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:07.040
Regional locations operate a
multi-zone replicated system,

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.250
whereas our multi-region
locations-- and not just

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.350
multi-zone, but
also multi-region

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:15.040
where we replicate
across distinct regions.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.940
Typically we would see people
picking a regional location

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:23.890
when they want to
co-locate it with, say,

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.630
a compute instance
they already have

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.600
in that particular location,
or they're exceptionally

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.155
sensitive to write latencies.

00:06:31.155 --> 00:06:33.280
If you're looking for
something that's more durable

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.900
and can survive
regional outages,

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.230
that's where we see the
majority of our customers

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.140
picking this
multi-region solution.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.340
For you, operationally,
there's no difference.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.590
It's simply a different
selection in a dropdown list.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.070
Now, I wanted to give you
a quick visually example.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.970
This is fairly simplified of
what our replication topology

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:59.390
looks like in one of our
multi-region locations.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:02.030
So this is US
central where we run

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:06.834
two full replicas in Oklahoma,
two full replicas in Iowa,

00:07:06.834 --> 00:07:08.750
and we also have what
we call a witness to act

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:12.780
as a tiebreaker
in South Carolina.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.310
Now, to connect
all these together,

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:18.230
we run three independent
fiber optic networks

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:20.420
so that we can have a
network down for maintenance,

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.250
we can have another
one unexpectedly out,

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:24.350
like someone goes through
it with a backhoe--

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.560
unfortunately, that
does sometimes happen--

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.050
and still continue to
serve full production load

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:31.490
without you noticing anything.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.140
The question was
are the two replicas

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:43.140
that I showed in each
region in different zones?

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.940
And the answer is
essentially yes.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.050
The last point in
this quick overview

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.500
on Cloud Dataflow still that
I wanted to give you is--

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.770
before we dumped down into some
of the more technical aspects--

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.170
is talking about the
key integration points

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:00.880
within the cloud platform.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.470
So obviously, you
can connect to it

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:04.060
from any of our
compute services,

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.570
whether it's App Engine
or Container Engine.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.000
We allow you to do
exports of your data

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.600
into cloud storage buckets
and import it back in.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.644
The same export format can be
imported into BigQuery as well.

00:08:18.644 --> 00:08:20.560
So if you want to do a
daily dump of your data

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.280
and then run SQL statements
over it in a data warehouse

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.920
solution, you can do that.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.670
The other one I really
wanted to point out

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:31.690
is the integration with Cloud
Dataflow flow or Apache beam.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.909
This will allow you to
do fan outs of reads

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:36.120
and writes across the
database in parallel,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.986
and is a really powerful
way to integrate

00:08:37.986 --> 00:08:39.235
into the rest of the platform.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.650
And that quick overview
out of the way,

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.380
let's go and have a look at
some common architectures

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:54.120
that we see our customers
using with Cloud Datastore.

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.980
To start off, I'm going to
show an example here of a more

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:58.795
traditional corporate system.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.790
Here what we're seeing is
a couple of compute VMs

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.590
that connect into
Cloud Datastore,

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:08.750
each handling a
different system.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.420
As an example, it could be
a sign-in system and maybe

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.180
a product catalog.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:19.120
What's really good about this
for the sign-in system is it

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.130
it's typically a spiky load.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.720
So most of the
time your database

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.260
would be sitting there at
90% idle over the week,

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:29.300
hardly being used except
for a couple of peak times.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.080
In Cloud Datastore
we only charge

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:35.440
you for the actual usage, the
calls to the database itself,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.790
not for having
capacity sit around.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.830
This is really handy
because over the weekend

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.410
you're not paying for
capacity you're not using.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:48.100
And when the signings start to
roll in begrudgingly on Monday

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.530
morning, we'll automatically
scale the system up

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:54.960
to handle that load spike, and
then go back to normal levels

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:55.650
during the week.

00:09:57.602 --> 00:09:59.810
The other thing I'm just
showing down at the bottom--

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.480
which I briefly
mentioned before--

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:06.400
is doing a daily export
into cloud storage, which

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.250
then gets imported into
BigQuery is that people

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:11.110
can run daily reports over it.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.240
And then you can use
standard SQL query

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.300
languages to
generate your reports

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.350
that the business needs.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.650
So with the Compute
Engine example,

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:23.560
that was a traditional system.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:26.130
Now I want to quickly
show you an example using

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.910
Container Engine, which is one
of the many configurations we

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.450
see people do in things like
mobile gaming platforms.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.820
So what you're seeing
here is pods set up

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:39.750
as a series of micro services.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:43.910
Between the pods and the
users is Google Cloud Lab.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:49.020
[AUDIO OUT] Cloud load balancing
to distribute the traffic

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:50.550
between the different pods.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.050
The first line of pods--

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.860
a play of front ends.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:56.640
So a player will have an
affinity to a particular pods.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.430
You can cache the data forum
for low latency serving.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.010
The next line of micro
service pods behind

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:06.280
that you could consider
a world backend.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.950
Now, these particular
pods have an affinity

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:14.050
to an area in the game world,
a particular match, or a level

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:15.430
that a player is playing.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:17.890
So once again, you
can cache state

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.350
that is local to that
particular aspect of the game

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:23.430
so you can have low latency.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.460
What you then have is
durable variety in that state

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.590
data down to data
stall, so you don't

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.360
have to worry about
the pods going down

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.537
and you can cycle new pods up
to take care of everything.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:42.160
The last quick example here
of an architecture we see

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.610
is using App Engine as this
fully managed scalable web app.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.920
You'll notice here we don't
have Google Cloud load balancer

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:54.580
because that's an automatic
feature of App Engine.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.920
We're also using
App Engine modules

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.250
to have isolation between
the different micro services

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.580
so you can handle
them independently.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:08.050
A little bit different here
is a module in App Engine

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:12.560
that we're showing writing
directly into BigQuery query

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.190
rather than using the
daily import and export.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:17.450
This is essentially a dual
write model for someone

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.370
who wants to have live data
in their data warehouse.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:24.380
For bigger deployments,
you will typically

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:28.280
see people putting Cloud
Pub/Sub in between the module

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.290
and BigQuery so
they can effectively

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.230
handle large amounts
of loads being streamed

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:35.255
into various BigQuery tables.

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:41.660
So let's go a little
bit deeper now and start

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.370
talking about how
you would Layout

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.765
your data in Cloud Datastore.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.750
First, a quick note on
some of the basic building

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:53.284
blocks you have available
and some of the terminology

00:12:53.284 --> 00:12:55.700
that we use because it's a
little bit different than you'd

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:57.180
normally see.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:58.700
In Cloud Datastore,
you can think

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.430
of what we call an
entity as essentially

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:04.480
a document or a table row.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:08.050
What we call a kind is sort of
like a collection or a table,

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.750
just a little bit more powerful.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:12.580
Now, there's a standard
list of data types

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.460
you can use, everything
from integers to blubs.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:17.420
There's also a slightly
more complex type

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:20.060
you can use, arrays
or embedded entities,

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:25.040
where embedded entities
are essentially just a map.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.370
You can also control
data locality

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.330
using hierarchies, something
that we call entity groups.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.520
Now, this particular
feature-- entity groups--

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.750
has implications on consistency
and write throughput

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:40.880
which we'll touch upon now.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:48.800
Strong consistency enables you
to have multiple applications

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.840
or people see a consistent
view of a database

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.760
in a particular time.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:58.630
This is really important
in distributed databases

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:00.790
for a variety of use cases.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:03.490
As an example, if you have
a social networking site,

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:08.920
you don't want to have a user
delete post p, add friend f,

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.680
and then have that friend
see the post that they just

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:13.870
deleted about their
surprise party because

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.751
of eventual consistency.

00:14:15.751 --> 00:14:17.500
So there's definitely
a strong requirement

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:19.450
for this in certain areas.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:23.020
There are also other areas
where strong consistency

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:24.200
isn't as needed.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.230
For example, if you're
trying to do a population

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:31.090
count across the US, if you're
off by a couple of births,

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.460
it doesn't really
make a difference.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:35.560
What you might be
looking for then,

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:41.350
instead, is having an ability
to have fast and scalable writes

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:42.880
across a large data set.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:45.170
So eventual consistency
can allow you to do that.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:50.140
A quick cheat to
think about this

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:52.900
as we go through a bit
more of this presentation

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:56.950
is to remember that
indexes in Cloud Datastore

00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:00.850
are eventually consistent,
whereas directly reading

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:05.030
the entity is always
strongly consistent.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:06.740
The one difference
here is if you

00:15:06.740 --> 00:15:09.950
use something called ancestor
queries that contain it

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.650
to a single entity
group, that also forces

00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:14.540
it to be strongly consistent.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:17.690
So how you lay out your data,
or how you query or read it,

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:21.080
allows you to sort of tune
and select the consistency

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.717
best of write throughput.

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.340
When we consult
with customers who

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:32.830
are writing very large
scale applications on Cloud

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:36.880
Datastore, I'll generally start
off with three main questions

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:39.670
to walk through to start
helping them understand

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:43.160
how to layout that data model.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.520
The first one is
understanding the queries

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.610
that you want to run
across your data.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:50.560
Cloud Datastore
forces you to use

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:55.900
queries that will scale by
the size of your results set,

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:58.240
not by the size
of your data set.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.700
So what that means is
if you have a query that

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:04.940
returns 10 entities, it will
take the same time to execute,

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.450
whether you have 100
entities in your database

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:11.050
or 100 million entities
in your database.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.860
That does mean we disallow
certain types of queries

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:19.320
to make sure that you're
only using queries at scale.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:24.180
The second question we
ask them is understanding

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:30.420
when your traffic increase, how
does your right profile change?

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.540
So we have some guideline
limits within the system.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.190
We have people sort of consider
when they're building it,

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.190
which helps understand
whether you build things

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.620
into entity groups or
spread it out wider and use

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:42.420
eventual consistency.

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.600
The first one is if you're
updating individual entities,

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:48.360
you want to try to keep it
to updating that entity only

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.129
once per second.

00:16:50.129 --> 00:16:52.170
So an example of where
you might think about that

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.390
is if you're trying
to do a counter.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.030
Doing a counter for every
person signing into your system

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.970
when you have 100
million concurrent users

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.305
is generally not a good
idea in any database.

00:17:03.305 --> 00:17:04.680
There are things
you can consider

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:07.470
like sharded counters
that will split

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:10.290
those updates across
a variety of entities

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:14.069
so you can effectively
increase your write throughput.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:19.500
The second limit is around
the entity group itself.

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:25.200
That also has a one transaction
per second write guideline

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:25.890
that we have.

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.290
Now, I said
transaction, not write,

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:31.530
because you can have multiple
entities within a transaction.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:33.600
You can batch hundreds
of entities together,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:39.530
and that gets considered
as one write transaction.

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:42.440
So you can do hundreds
of entities per seconds

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:44.930
into that group, but you have
to make sure you batch it

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:48.170
appropriately.

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:50.920
The next one, in terms
of guideline limits

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:54.700
when looking at how your
write scale with your traffic,

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.760
is any place you might have
monotonically increasing

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.370
or decreasing keys.

00:18:00.370 --> 00:18:04.660
The way our system splits
or shards out data,

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:07.150
and then splits the
servers that handle it,

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:10.750
means that we can
sustain about 500 writes

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.570
per second on sequential data.

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:16.780
We'll see in a second how
we lay out indexes on disk,

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:18.790
but the essential
implication of this

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:21.640
is the same thing applies
for sequential data.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.250
So if you're trying to index
a particular property that

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:27.130
is a time stamp now,
you want to keep

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:30.070
your writes per second
on that particular kind

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:32.930
under 500 per second.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:35.560
Now, when you keep
that in mind, it

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.060
is worth noting you
can do a million write

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:41.330
per second or more across
the entire database

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:46.810
as long as you have your
data set up appropriately.

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:49.420
It's also good to note here
a general rule if you've ever

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:52.450
talked to a team that works
on Cloud Datastores, what

00:18:52.450 --> 00:18:55.600
they like to call the 555 rule.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:57.610
This is when you're
ramping up traffic

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:58.960
for the first time with writes.

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:01.810
You want to look at starting
at a base write rate of 500

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:06.830
writes per second, increasing
it by 50% every five minutes.

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:08.860
So using that type
of ramp up model

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:11.570
you can get to
about 700,000 writes

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.630
for second in under 90 minutes.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:17.950
And it's just important to have
that constant ramp up profile

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:20.620
to make sure that we split
the system and scale up

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.720
appropriately.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:27.130
The last question that we ask
is about consistency guarantees

00:19:27.130 --> 00:19:28.480
that you need.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:30.970
Just remembering that look
ups and ancestor queries

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:32.800
are strongly
consistent, and other

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:37.382
queries that use indexes
are eventually consistent.

00:19:37.382 --> 00:19:39.840
So I'm going to dive into the
weeds for just a second here,

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.470
just to give you an example of
where understanding what type

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:46.430
of query you're doing can change
how you lay out your data.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:48.320
So just for a
second, let's suppose

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.330
you have an employee
system that wants

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:54.890
to store the start and end
times of someone's shift

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:57.234
over the week.

00:19:57.234 --> 00:19:59.150
The first way you might
think about doing this

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:01.430
is storing two
arrays of start times

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:04.580
in the appropriate and times.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:07.430
Noting from the previous
slide, the types of queries

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:12.260
we support are multiple quality
filters, multiple quality

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.840
filters with one
inequality filter,

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:17.750
but we don't support
too inequality filters

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:19.670
on different properties.

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:22.790
So if you think that we have
one array of start times, one

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:26.720
array of end times, you'd have
to do two inequality filters

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:29.210
on different properties,
which doesn't actually

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:32.880
scale for very large loads.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.400
You could change how you do
your data, though, and instead

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:38.280
of storing start
time and end times,

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:41.850
break the day up into
30 minute periods,

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:43.860
and then store each
30 minute block

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.440
that that person is on shift.

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.690
Now, if you account for the
day of the week in that,

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.610
that changes you're
two inequality

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.670
filters to a single
equality filter, which

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:55.650
is a very scalable model.

00:20:55.650 --> 00:20:57.510
So just below the
data model here

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.430
is just a small
snippet of JavaScript

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:02.640
that shows you how you'd convert
the time into 30 minute blocks.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:04.630
And then the query
itself is very simple.

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:06.990
You just use the
Query Builder in Java,

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.537
set the kind to employees, and
then filter by that 30 minute

00:21:10.537 --> 00:21:12.120
block that you're
looking for, and you

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.850
can find everyone whose
on shift right now

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:16.420
and invite them to
the pizza party.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:22.890
Another example
is what if you're

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:24.420
writing a game review site?

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:29.350
How would you lay out your
data to do reviews on games?

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.270
So obviously, here
you would start off

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:33.405
with a table or a
kind called games,

00:21:33.405 --> 00:21:34.780
and you give it
a bunch of data--

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:37.960
some auto ID as the primary key.

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:40.840
You'd have a string for the
game name and description.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:44.170
You might have a link to an
image within GCS for the title

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.450
or cover art.

00:21:46.450 --> 00:21:49.090
How would you lay
out the reviews?

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:53.050
Well, one option here
that in strong consistency

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:56.036
is to use those entity
groups we mentioned before.

00:21:56.036 --> 00:21:58.660
So within your review, where you
have a rating and the user who

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:01.900
submitted it, you would
set an ancestor key

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:03.230
for the game itself.

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:05.890
And this would force that
into the same location

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:06.620
on the server.

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:10.820
And then you can do strongly
consistent queries against it.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:12.800
Generally, that would
work well, but if you're

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:15.517
trying to launch a
really large scale game,

00:22:15.517 --> 00:22:17.600
and you're worried about
the number of new reviews

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:22.760
per second exceeding one write
per second into that entity

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:24.350
group for the game,
you might need

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:28.100
to think about using your data
model a little bit differently.

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:30.170
So just a quick
disclaimer on that one

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:32.270
write transaction
that, as I said,

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:33.950
it's more like a guideline.

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:35.635
So you can definitely
burst above that

00:22:35.635 --> 00:22:38.930
to like 30 writes per second
for short periods of time.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:40.970
Eventually, if you keep
a high right right,

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:44.360
though, you'll exhaust our
internal cues in the system.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:46.190
At that point, we'll
start rejecting

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:49.820
the new write you do with
essentially a rate limit.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:50.990
But you can bust above it.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:53.390
But say you're concerned
that a new game is

00:22:53.390 --> 00:22:54.494
going to be super popular.

00:22:54.494 --> 00:22:55.910
I don't know, maybe
you're running

00:22:55.910 --> 00:23:00.127
around the world catching these
little critters in an AR game.

00:23:00.127 --> 00:23:01.460
I don't know what that would be.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:03.030
It sounds like a
good idea, though.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:04.696
So if you're doing
that, you're probably

00:23:04.696 --> 00:23:07.610
going to end up with more than
one game review per second

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.960
for an extended period of time.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:12.140
So instead of doing
this ancestor model,

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:14.390
you can choose the second
model I'm showing here where

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:15.650
we change it up a little bit.

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:18.080
We actually store the game
idea as a regular property

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:19.460
that gets indexed.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.610
So now I can do a query
that's eventually consistent.

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.410
But I can do as many rights
as I want because they're not

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.217
in entity groups.

00:23:28.217 --> 00:23:30.300
That generally works really
well for game reviews,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.850
except for one edge case,
which can be really jarring,

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:36.720
and that's when a user writes a
review, goes to the game list,

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:39.000
and doesn't see
their own review.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:40.590
That's a pretty bad experience.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:42.166
So what we've done
here to get around

00:23:42.166 --> 00:23:44.040
it is a little bit of
a cheat to make it look

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.960
like it's strongly consistent.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:48.690
And the ID, instead
have been an auto ID,

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:52.780
is actually the combination of
the game ID and the user ID.

00:23:52.780 --> 00:23:55.710
So now when you're generating
your list of game reviews,

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:57.600
you do your eventually
consistent query

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.820
to get all game
reviews, and then you

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:03.660
do a look up on the game ID
with the current user's ID.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:05.370
Because look ups are
strongly consistent,

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:09.970
you're always guaranteed to see
that particular user's review.

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:13.020
So now you have a write
rate where you can have

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:14.980
millions of reviews per second.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:17.010
And to any regular
user, it's going

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:18.925
to look like it's
strongly consistent.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:24.430
OK.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:27.620
Now time to go a
little bit deeper.

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:30.280
I want to share out how this
data that we just talked about

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:34.107
would actually be laid out on
Datastore so you can understand

00:24:34.107 --> 00:24:35.440
what's happening under the hood.

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:40.630
As you may or may not be
aware, Cloud Datastore

00:24:40.630 --> 00:24:44.030
is built on top of
mega-store and Bigtable.

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:45.799
So if you want to
read more about how

00:24:45.799 --> 00:24:47.840
that some of the underlying
infrastructure works,

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.340
we have research
papers you can go

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:52.910
read if you're interested
in that sort of thing.

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:55.400
Mega-store and Bigtable
are the two technologies

00:24:55.400 --> 00:24:57.770
we build on top of, and we
add a bunch of functionality

00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:00.030
on top of that.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:02.520
So to start with here we
have sort of a primary table

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:05.030
called entities where we store
all the entities that you're

00:25:05.030 --> 00:25:07.320
writing into the database.

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:09.570
That's generally structured
where the key you provide

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:13.490
is combined with your project
ID, the name space, the kind--

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.840
all gets combined
together as primary key.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.000
And then in each
of the columns we

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:19.860
have all the data
you've written.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.540
And BigTable is a
wide column store.

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:23.550
We essentially just
use it for a key value

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:26.700
store here with the key
and the data review entity.

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:30.120
Well, we also have a kind
index, so we can query by that.

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:32.550
Any of your secondary
indexes, any time you

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:35.190
index an individual property,
we also write them out

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:37.410
into their own column families.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:39.910
And the way we do that
is we take the data out

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:41.610
of the property you're
indexing and make

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:45.790
that the key combined with
things like your project ID.

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:48.000
And then the data,
the value, is the key

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:51.490
of the entity we're looking for.

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:55.720
We also for built-in properties,
these secondary indexes,

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:59.494
we index it both ascending
and descending order.

00:25:59.494 --> 00:26:00.910
So the thing to
keep in mind there

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:04.330
is any time you turn on an
index for a property, that's

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:06.490
two index entries,
one for ascending,

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:09.650
one for the descending.

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:11.730
So moving on to a
little more complex

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:14.194
feature here where we
have composite indexes.

00:26:14.194 --> 00:26:15.610
This is essentially
where you say,

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:19.570
I want a query using
multiple properties

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:20.530
within the data model.

00:26:20.530 --> 00:26:22.600
So you tell us to
create a composite index

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.437
of those particular properties.

00:26:24.437 --> 00:26:26.770
As you might have guessed
from the previous one, the way

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:28.300
we do that is we
essentially just

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:32.020
combine the two property
values together, and then have

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.430
the value as the key as well.

00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:36.880
Now, this one here you have
to tell us the sort order

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:38.830
because we will do it
ascending and descending

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.770
for each property, and depending
on the way you want to query it

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:43.480
or the sort order
that you use, we'll

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:45.890
need to have a different
index built for that.

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:53.050
And then the third one here,
with my very bad illustration

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.372
skills, you would
see in property C,

00:26:55.372 --> 00:26:56.830
I've tried to
indicate that there's

00:26:56.830 --> 00:26:58.780
an essentially an array there.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:02.320
We have three different values
that we store in an array.

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:05.510
When we create an index for
an array, we explode it out,

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:08.895
and we create an index
entry for every array value.

00:27:08.895 --> 00:27:11.020
This is [INAUDIBLE] you
need to keep in mind if you

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:15.746
try to do a composite index
on multiple properties that

00:27:15.746 --> 00:27:18.370
are indexes because you're going
to have the cardinal property,

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:21.930
and it's going to explode to
two arrays of three properties

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.010
as now nine index values.

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:26.080
You can very quickly end
up with a large amount

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:28.750
of indexes we're writing.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:30.970
This is important when
you're doing any query that's

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:32.540
eventually consistent.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:33.970
So as I mentioned
before, queries

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:35.980
are eventually consistent.

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:38.830
Sorry, indexes are
eventually consistent.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:42.130
And the amount of explosion
of indexes you have per entity

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:45.010
is going to affect how many
milliseconds it takes us

00:27:45.010 --> 00:27:47.410
in the average case
to bring that back

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:50.220
to a strongly consistent state.

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:56.650
Now, I'm going to jump back to
that entity table I mentioned

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:58.300
before, and just
quickly highlight

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:02.980
how we split up or shard
the data to be served.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:04.810
Much like BigTable,
we essentially

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:10.210
use horizontal row splitting
to work out which server

00:28:10.210 --> 00:28:12.590
handles which range of data.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:16.150
So we'll do a horizontal range
handled by a single server.

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:19.690
As the load increases on
any one of these servers

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:22.390
that we call tablets,
we'll essentially

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:25.570
split that horizontal
range in two,

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:27.730
and then have one server
handle the left hand

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:31.616
side and another server
handle the right hand side.

00:28:31.616 --> 00:28:34.240
This goes back to what I talked
about with the sequential data,

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:37.034
the monotonically increasing
or decreasing keys.

00:28:37.034 --> 00:28:38.950
So when you think about
how we split the data,

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:42.610
or if you're sequentially adding
data to the end of a table,

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:44.980
when we split, we're going
to have one server handle

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:47.960
all the same amount of
load, and the other server

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:49.930
is going to handle no load.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:55.910
So that's why we don't generally
like indexing sequential data.

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:58.280
There is another
case here, and that's

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.770
when we're talking
about a single key.

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:03.440
So if you're trying to
do a lot to a single key,

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:06.410
it's very hard to
shard a single key.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:08.240
In fact, I think
it's impossible.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.050
Yes.

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:11.730
WILL HAYWORTH: Alas.

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:13.110
DAN MCGRATH:
Generally, that will

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:15.330
lead to a quite
sensible question

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:18.870
that people ask next, what
about low cardinality data?

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:21.330
I have a property that's
just true or false.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.000
Does that mean you can't
scale by that data?

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.640
Well, under the covers we
have a nifty little trick

00:29:26.640 --> 00:29:30.030
that we call a scatter key,
which is a two bite randomized

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:34.410
value that we append to
every value that we buy,

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.870
whether it's the primary
key of your entity itself

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:38.470
or the index data.

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:41.220
What that means is every
time we write data,

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.350
if it's low cardinality,
we will randomly

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:47.700
distributed it so that we can
adequately split on that data.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.070
That doesn't work for
monotonically increasing

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:53.490
or decreasing data because
a random value at the end

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:56.640
is always at the end
so it doesn't help us.

00:30:00.869 --> 00:30:02.410
And so with that
I'm going to move on

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:04.256
to load testing,
which is probably

00:30:04.256 --> 00:30:06.880
an important thing, even though
we're a fully managed solution,

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:08.920
we'll automatically scale
up and down for you,

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:10.840
there are a lot of
gotchas that when you're

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:13.180
running at low scale
you may not realize

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:14.380
you've inadvertently done.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:17.480
Sequential data is
one of those examples.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:19.840
So when you're
doing load testing,

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:22.030
you're really looking for
a couple of things here.

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:24.100
And the key one here
is hot spotting.

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:25.510
So that's the
example where we're

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:27.670
talking about we can't
split in the correct way

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:29.570
to handle your data.

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:32.440
And we'll have a
particular row that

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.930
may be hot that has
brought down applications

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:37.710
before where they're
trying to do 100,000

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.280
reads from a single row.

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:41.910
And unfortunately, a single
server struggles to serve that.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:47.540
One thing to think
about when you're

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:49.340
talking about load
testing is very

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:51.950
different from the
traditional on-prem problems

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:53.150
you would have.

00:30:53.150 --> 00:30:56.900
So generally, when you're
trying to do very large load

00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:59.660
tests with on-prem, you need
to order hardware for it,

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:01.910
and that can be
very hard to justify

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:04.250
spending enough hardware
to bring your application

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:05.566
to the point where it breaks.

00:31:05.566 --> 00:31:07.190
And you can understand
where the stress

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:10.010
points are in that application.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.500
When you're running in
most of the cloud models,

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.480
this can actually
be very effective

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:17.120
because you only pay for the
resources when you're using it.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:20.090
With something like GCA, 10
minutes minimum for the VM,

00:31:20.090 --> 00:31:21.620
and then you pay by the minute.

00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:24.540
The moment you finish using
it, you don't need to do it.

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:27.644
So we've had customers
on Cloud Datastore who--

00:31:27.644 --> 00:31:29.060
with our guidance
because they are

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.110
running at such large scales--

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:33.140
will sit down and,
for an hour, they

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:36.320
will do a 1 million requests
per second load test,

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:39.380
a 2 million requests
per second load test,

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:42.800
a 3 million requests
per second load test,

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.170
and then they find a fairly
point in their algorithm

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:48.610
where an n squared
algorithm finally hits them.

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:50.300
It's not just data
so you're looking

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:53.050
at when you're doing load and
performance testing as well.

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:55.550
I specifically say
performance because latency

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:58.040
can have really odd
effects when you're talking

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:00.320
about applications at scale.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:03.380
And this is sort of cascading
failure that you typically

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:06.080
see in large
applications where when

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.810
you put a certain area
of the system under load,

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:11.090
that keeps up but
at higher latency.

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:12.980
Something that took
10 milliseconds, now

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:15.290
takes 100, 200 milliseconds.

00:32:15.290 --> 00:32:18.440
And that causes the next part
of the system to back up,

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:21.530
which then adds load
onto the next section.

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:24.590
An example here would be
if you're using Pub/Sub

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:28.349
to batch large amounts of writes
together into an entity group.

00:32:28.349 --> 00:32:29.640
Generally, this is going along.

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:32.780
And it's only writing 10
to 20 entities per second

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:35.120
into an entity group.

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.920
If the system
slows down in being

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:38.930
able to pull all these
entities out, batch them

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:41.040
together, and write
them into the system,

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.270
you might start seeing
the batch size increase.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:47.560
So you're going from 10 to
20 entities per second being

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.600
batched together to
100, 200 entities being

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:52.040
written in a single batch.

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:53.660
And we will keep up with that.

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:56.270
At some point, you
will get to the area

00:32:56.270 --> 00:32:59.287
where we have a 10
megabyte transaction limit.

00:32:59.287 --> 00:33:01.370
So all of a sudden your
application's going along,

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:02.810
you don't see anything wrong.

00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:06.740
And then, bam, you hit a 10
megabite per transaction limit,

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:09.170
and your writes
to Datastore stop.

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:11.840
Now, it's not because any
particular area wasn't keeping

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:13.700
up, but this
cascading effect that

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:15.470
ended up with larger
batches until it

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:16.965
got to that critical point.

00:33:16.965 --> 00:33:19.340
So that's why, whenever you're
trying to launch something

00:33:19.340 --> 00:33:21.173
that's large and you
haven't done it before,

00:33:21.173 --> 00:33:24.290
is really important to stress
the system beyond what you

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:26.480
think the max is going to be.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:29.180
And then you can find these
particular stress points,

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:31.510
and fix them before
your live in production

00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:35.056
because that's definitely
a more stressful time.

00:33:35.056 --> 00:33:37.160
So a little bit.

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:39.760
So with that, I'm
going to hand off

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:44.140
to Will who will take you
through a fun little demo.

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:46.130
WILL HAYWORTH: Hi, everyone.

00:33:46.130 --> 00:33:48.340
Thanks, Dan, for that
really awesome exploration

00:33:48.340 --> 00:33:50.890
of Datastore internals.

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:52.630
So don't get your hopes up.

00:33:52.630 --> 00:33:54.820
This is actually a
really simple game.

00:33:54.820 --> 00:33:57.640
But what's a workshop about
building scalable apps

00:33:57.640 --> 00:34:01.330
if it doesn't actually include
some sort of scalable app,

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:03.190
right?

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:05.380
So we have a small
example for you.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:08.179
It's Rock, Paper, Scissors.

00:34:08.179 --> 00:34:10.699
You're all so excited.

00:34:10.699 --> 00:34:13.561
So this is--
especially, I mean, I

00:34:13.561 --> 00:34:15.560
don't know if you saw me
off to the side earlier

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:17.270
finishing up the last
bit of JavaScript,

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:19.760
which I haven't written
in a very long time.

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:21.889
This is more of a
concept than something

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:23.030
we've actually built out.

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:25.940
But these components you can
find in real life systems.

00:34:25.940 --> 00:34:29.120
One of the nice things about
building on top App Engine,

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:31.100
building on top of
Datastore and, in fact,

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:34.790
on GCP in general, is that these
underlying components scale

00:34:34.790 --> 00:34:37.610
by default. They encourage
scalable patterns

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:38.920
of application development.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.400
And in particular,
as well explore

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:43.909
when we get to the data model
of Rock, Paper, Scissors,

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:47.030
it's actually very easy to do
the right thing by default.

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:52.380
So to start here's the
QR code, and a short URL.

00:34:52.380 --> 00:34:54.290
Go on.

00:34:54.290 --> 00:34:57.950
Sorry, if you want to, that is.

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:00.740
You'll find that a page
led to some JavaScript.

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:05.020
And actually explore the
architecture in a second, wow,

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:07.030
sound like a submarine.

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:08.360
And go ahead.

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:09.410
Tap it.

00:35:09.410 --> 00:35:09.970
Tap.

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:13.440
You'll see buttons, a
space for your name.

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:14.040
All right.

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:14.580
Anybody?

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:15.301
Anybody winning?

00:35:15.301 --> 00:35:15.800
Losing?

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:19.350
Not seeing anything?

00:35:22.188 --> 00:35:25.430
[SIGH] This is why we
can't go to Vegas together.

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:27.890
Sorry.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:30.980
By the way, Avalanche is the
AI, in case you're-- not,

00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:31.760
not my name.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:33.830
Dan's name for it,
which is kind of nice.

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:37.030
Makes it sound like
a secret agent.

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:37.540
All right.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:39.430
So is somebody seeing results?

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:40.390
Please.

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:41.200
Anyone?

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:43.270
OK.

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:46.690
So this is actually what that
looks like under the hood,

00:35:46.690 --> 00:35:47.710
right?

00:35:47.710 --> 00:35:50.910
The purchases
obviously, matchmaking,

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:51.910
are somewhat conceptual.

00:35:51.910 --> 00:35:54.160
But in fact, the rest
of this is quite real.

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:57.850
So this particular Rock
Paper Scissors app,

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.860
which we've called
Rochamboogle, is

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:01.810
built on top of Cloud
Endpoints, which

00:36:01.810 --> 00:36:05.260
is a serverless API management
and enablement framework that

00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:07.895
builds on top of App
Engine standard itself,

00:36:07.895 --> 00:36:09.520
in addition to some
other API gateways,

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:10.960
but that's how
we're using it here.

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:13.270
In effect, it means that our
app is actually serverless.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:15.103
And if you were to take
a look at the source

00:36:15.103 --> 00:36:17.590
code for this particular
Rock, Paper, Scissors game,

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:19.000
you would actually
see that we're

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:21.190
using auto generated
client libraries, generated

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:24.730
by the endpoints framework
itself, which is pretty cool.

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:26.400
So that's server list, right?

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:28.680
So where's Cloud Datastore?

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.390
We hit Cloud
Datastore, obviously,

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:32.160
within App Engine itself.

00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:36.270
But we actually scale up
in our data model per user

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:37.620
and per game.

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:39.594
So there's actually
no concrete limit

00:36:39.594 --> 00:36:42.010
to how far we can scale, short
of what Dan mentioned early

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:46.284
with respect to the 555 rule.

00:36:46.284 --> 00:36:47.450
I think that's right, right?

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:48.201
555?

00:36:48.201 --> 00:36:48.700
Yeah.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:50.610
Awesome.

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:54.090
As you can see, Datastore
via a managed back up process

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:56.910
can go easily to
cloud storage, which

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:58.830
would allow us to have
a beautiful dashboards

00:36:58.830 --> 00:36:59.790
and BigQuery.

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:00.300
Hooray.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:02.400
Maybe you've heard about it.

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:05.000
And-- yeah.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:06.140
Let's take a look.

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:08.556
The data model itself is--

00:37:08.556 --> 00:37:09.710
this is NDB code.

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:14.530
NDB is the premier App Engine--

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:15.980
ORM for Python, basically.

00:37:15.980 --> 00:37:18.670
It was actually co-written
by Guido van Rossum

00:37:18.670 --> 00:37:20.360
when he was on the team.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:22.870
As you can see, we use up a
bunch of different properties

00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:23.370
here.

00:37:23.370 --> 00:37:24.410
One of the nice things
about this library

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:25.790
is that actually lets us--

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:28.940
it handles the
denormalization for us.

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:33.130
So Dan talked about
entity groups earlier.

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:35.100
If we look at the
user in particular,

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:36.600
this doesn't
actually-- this doesn't

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:38.490
have the user key on it.

00:37:38.490 --> 00:37:43.220
But the key itself, if you
look at the app itself,

00:37:43.220 --> 00:37:44.760
is just a string value.

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:47.040
Assuming that that's actually
randomly distributed,

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:49.560
it's really easy to
grow that, as your app

00:37:49.560 --> 00:37:55.830
grows from one user to 100
users to a million users.

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:58.170
As long as each one
is a different string,

00:37:58.170 --> 00:38:00.294
or a different ID,
that's the scalable--

00:38:00.294 --> 00:38:02.460
that's the scalability and
the underlying mega-store

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:03.690
app itself.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:06.780
The game itself
has the same issue

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:09.420
where it actually uses a
randomly assigned integer

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:10.380
instead.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:14.010
But that's all handled for us
by Datastore under the hood.

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:18.245
Furthermore, it's really easy--

00:38:18.245 --> 00:38:19.620
you know, consistency
can be kind

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.590
of a hairy area in
distributed databases, right?

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:25.297
One of the things that
would naturally come up

00:38:25.297 --> 00:38:27.630
when you're trying to update
something like a statistics

00:38:27.630 --> 00:38:29.796
property, is that if you're
trying to increment say,

00:38:29.796 --> 00:38:33.630
wins or losses from the result
of a match, what happens if you

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:35.130
perform dirty reads, right?

00:38:35.130 --> 00:38:35.797
Or dirty writes?

00:38:35.797 --> 00:38:37.588
I want to say that's
the other term for it.

00:38:37.588 --> 00:38:38.580
Yeah.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:40.830
In fact, what happens
if we actually

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:43.440
increment data that has
been committed, right,

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:45.310
or we lose data?

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:47.070
So NDB actually handles this.

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:50.640
But in the process, it builds
on top of the underlying Cloud

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:52.590
Datastore transactional
framework.

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:56.430
Because to commit the game,
we need to update the users.

00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:58.380
So these are three
discrete entities.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:02.130
Datastore can actually handle up
to 25 entities in transaction.

00:39:02.130 --> 00:39:03.930
Creating a transaction
like that can

00:39:03.930 --> 00:39:05.560
be something of a bottleneck.

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:07.390
But it does work well.

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:09.510
And the semantics are
actually serializable,

00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:13.500
which is kind of cool
for a NoSQL datastore.

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:18.620
So this is what it looks
like to actually use

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:19.610
of a veneer library.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:21.360
The underlying thing
is written in Python.

00:39:21.360 --> 00:39:24.354
This actually, sorry, veneer
is our term for our REST APIs

00:39:24.354 --> 00:39:26.270
that you can access in
a variety of languages,

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:27.890
from Python to Go.

00:39:27.890 --> 00:39:30.452
This is using that library.

00:39:30.452 --> 00:39:31.910
As you can see,
it's really simple.

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:33.441
You assign a key--

00:39:33.441 --> 00:39:35.690
the key part, should you
choose to design it yourself,

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:38.660
is where you need to watch for
hot spotting issues, right?

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:43.580
The other parts are the data
store you know and love, either

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:45.350
from your usage of
an object model,

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:47.210
or from using it on
App Engine before.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:52.840
Getting the data out of
it it's just as easy.

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:56.830
We do a query on the kind
because that's the collection

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:58.600
of the underlined entities.

00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:00.280
And we do a read from it.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:02.580
This is how one does it
on top of the Dataflow

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:04.290
or now Apache Beam SDK.

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:09.990
Once we have that, we
just apply that read,

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:14.910
and well, as you can see, we
have different maps and a sink.

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:17.070
And the nice part is that
Datastore will actually

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:18.870
manage that sharding for us.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:20.580
Again, assuming
that IDs have been

00:40:20.580 --> 00:40:22.950
assigned in a roughly
uniform distribution,

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:24.235
you should be good to go.

00:40:24.235 --> 00:40:26.610
And you don't have to care,
even when your app goes viral

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:27.447
overnight.

00:40:31.030 --> 00:40:33.530
Endpoints is really
neat, by the way.

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:35.960
One of the advantages
of it is that should you

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:38.570
choose to build on top of end
points, on top of App Engine,

00:40:38.570 --> 00:40:40.370
on top of Datastore
you don't have

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:41.960
to worry about
provisioning servers

00:40:41.960 --> 00:40:45.950
ever, which is really nice
because, as we all know,

00:40:45.950 --> 00:40:47.780
scale happens.

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:50.950
Again, sometimes overnight.

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:53.110
Finally, some kinds
of data you don't

00:40:53.110 --> 00:40:54.820
want to store in Datastore.

00:40:54.820 --> 00:40:58.390
For this, we have Cloud Storage.

00:40:58.390 --> 00:40:59.962
Cloud Storage has
a CDN built-in.

00:40:59.962 --> 00:41:01.420
That's really nice
if you're trying

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:05.080
to serve a lot of things
like, say, that configuration

00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:07.900
value that need-- that you might
ordinarily store in a database,

00:41:07.900 --> 00:41:10.420
but you could easily
overload by a hot key.

00:41:10.420 --> 00:41:12.550
You can store it in
Datastore instead.

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:14.470
I'm sorry, in
Storage, excuse me.

00:41:14.470 --> 00:41:16.300
And it's globally distributed.

00:41:16.300 --> 00:41:18.280
And remember how we
talked about splitting?

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:21.070
Well, you can hammer
this thing with a key

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:23.320
because it's a cache
under the hood.

00:41:23.320 --> 00:41:25.237
It's a blob storage device.

00:41:25.237 --> 00:41:27.070
So you don't have to
worry about overloading

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:33.360
the underlying Bigtable tablet
the way you would in Datastore.

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:35.355
Anyone having fun?

00:41:35.355 --> 00:41:36.730
I know the game
is a little lame.

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:40.550
But it's-- but the thing is, if
you guys are playing this right

00:41:40.550 --> 00:41:44.152
now, and we told everybody
else in this building about it,

00:41:44.152 --> 00:41:45.860
we actually wouldn't
have to do anything.

00:41:45.860 --> 00:41:48.110
I mean, we might make
a couple SREs nervous.

00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:50.750
But beyond that, the
underlying architecture

00:41:50.750 --> 00:41:52.460
has been proven to scale.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:59.372
It does millions of QPS, and we
can piggyback on that for free.

00:41:59.372 --> 00:41:59.955
So with that--

00:42:06.080 --> 00:42:08.610
DAN MCGRATH: I just want
to make this official.

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:13.160
Can you read out my outcomes?

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:14.090
WILL HAYWORTH: Draw.

00:42:14.090 --> 00:42:15.020
Draw.

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:16.415
Draw.

00:42:16.415 --> 00:42:17.700
Draw.

00:42:17.700 --> 00:42:19.330
Lose.

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:22.940
DAN MCGRATH: I'm really
not good at this game.

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:23.960
WILL HAYWORTH: Yeah.

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:24.770
That Vegas thing.

00:42:27.434 --> 00:42:28.100
DAN MCGRATH: OK.

00:42:28.100 --> 00:42:29.141
So we already mentioned--

00:42:29.141 --> 00:42:32.270
I just wanted to put the
link back up there again--

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:33.850
once again, very
simplistic demo.

00:42:33.850 --> 00:42:35.600
But we just wanted to
give you an example.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:39.200
If this here just happens to
get as popular as Pokemons Go,

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:41.810
we can still be on this stage
and not have to do anything.

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:44.930
It will-- the infrastructure
will do it itself.

00:42:44.930 --> 00:42:47.390
If an entire region
goes down, we still

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:49.230
don't need to do
anything because it's

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:53.700
in a multi-region
replication configuration.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.240
So if you haven't looked into
it before, go to the website,

00:42:57.240 --> 00:42:59.210
cloud.Google.com/Datastore.

00:42:59.210 --> 00:43:00.710
Read up.

00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:01.590
Play with it.

00:43:01.590 --> 00:43:04.000
We have a free tier
within Cloud Datatore

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:06.770
so you can do 50,000
reads per day,

00:43:06.770 --> 00:43:10.100
20,000 writes 20,000 deletes
and a gigabyte of storage

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:11.450
without having to pay anything.

00:43:11.450 --> 00:43:13.190
So it's a really
good way to just play

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:15.200
around and get used to it.

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:17.994
You can look on GitHub for
some of those client libraries

00:43:17.994 --> 00:43:19.160
that we've mentioned before.

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:24.560
It's built on top of our
RESTful API, an our GRPC API.

00:43:24.560 --> 00:43:28.490
So you just do Google Cloud
Java, Google Cloud Python.

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:30.230
Look for that on
GitHub, and you'll

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:32.060
get the appropriate language.

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:38.960
We do Python, Java Go,
Node.js, Ruby, C Sharp.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:42.580
And I think we have
PHP now as well.

00:43:42.580 --> 00:43:44.060
We're also on Stack Overflow.

00:43:44.060 --> 00:43:46.700
You'll see myself, Will,
and a bunch of other people.

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:49.140
We'll jump on there
and answer questions.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.950
And you can also hit us up on
